@meta
id: 10-14
title: Inside the object literal
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-brace, obj-paren
par: 62
minutes: 6
vscode: select between the braces and try not to eat one of them
open: order.repository.ts

@brief
`i{` is everything between a matching pair of curly braces, and `a{` takes
the braces too. They behave exactly like the round-bracket pair, which is
the point — once you know one delimiter object you know all of them.

In TypeScript curly braces mean an object literal or a block, and the
first of those is where `ci{` earns its keep: a Prisma `where` clause, a
set of options, a props object.

The `deleted` column was dropped in the migration that went out on
Tuesday and both queries in this repository still filter on it. Change
them:

  - `findForCustomer` filters on the customer id it was given
  - `findOpen` filters on `status: 'PENDING'`

The outer `{ where: ... }` wrapper stays in both. Only the inner object
changes, and it keeps its spacing: `{ customerId }`, not `{customerId}`.

@keys
| `i{` | everything between the braces, spaces included |
| `ci{` | change it |
| `iB` `aB` | the same objects without the shift key |
| `a{` | the braces as well |

@hints
There are two pairs of braces on each of those lines and you want the
inner one. Vim picks the innermost pair containing the cursor.
--
`ci{` changes what is between the braces. Note what `i{` includes: the
space after `{` and the space before `}` are part of the object, so type
them back.
--
`2f{` lands on the inner opening brace. Then `ci{` and type a space, the
filter, and a space.

@solution
    6j 2f{ ci{  customerId  <Esc>
    4j 0 2f{ ci{  status: 'PENDING'  <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Fifty-four keystrokes. The two leading and trailing spaces in the typed
text are not a mistake in the solution listing — `i{` runs from just after
the opening brace to just before the closing one, so whatever padding the
file had is yours to retype.

That is worth knowing before it surprises you in a diff. If you want the
padding handled for you, target the outer object instead and retype more,
or use `ci{` and start your typing with a space out of habit. Most people
end up with the habit.

`B` for the shifted version is the same idea as `b` for round brackets:
`iB` is `i{`, `aB` is `a{`. On a UK or US keyboard `ciB` is three
unshifted keys, which over a day of editing is a real saving.

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord> {
  public async findForCustomer(customerId: string) {
    return this.model.findMany({ where: { deleted: false } });
  }

  public async findOpen() {
    return this.model.findMany({ where: { deleted: false } });
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord> {
  public async findForCustomer(customerId: string) {
    return this.model.findMany({ where: { customerId } });
  }

  public async findOpen() {
    return this.model.findMany({ where: { status: 'PENDING' } });
  }
}
